---
/**
 * The phone half of the side-navigation prototype: the tab list as a drawer.
 *
 * **Inline, for the same reason `DashTabsBoot` is inline.** In the top-strip layout the tabs are
 * on screen and a tap works during parse. Behind a drawer they are not, so if opening it waited on
 * the dashboard's ~61 KB bundle, the whole navigation of this page would be dead for as long as
 * that download takes — which is the exact failure that script was written for (owner,
 * 2026-07-31: *"הכל תקוע"*). Zero network, runs during parse, works from the first paint.
 *
 * It owns the BEHAVIOUR and the scrim, and nothing else: open/close, `aria-expanded`, Escape, the
 * focus trap while open, closing on a tab choice, and carrying the loudest alert marker out of the
 * closed drawer onto the trigger.
 *
 * **The trigger itself is in the site header** (`Header.astro`, `dashNav`), which is why nothing
 * here renders a button. It was a full-width bar inside the card until 2026-08-23, and that cost a
 * row it could not earn: it named the open tab, which the panel's own sticky title already says
 * one line below, and it pushed that title down under itself (owner: *"הפס עם ההמבורגר במובייל
 * צריך להיעלם. הוא תופס שורה שלמה, יוצר כותרת כפולה"*). A header that is already `position: fixed`
 * carries an icon at no vertical cost and is pinned by construction — no `position: sticky` whose
 * containing block has to be reasoned about, which is what the two previous attempts got wrong.
 *
 * Rendered only by the side layout (`?nav=top` omits it), and the trigger only paints below the
 * rail's breakpoint — above it the rail is simply there.
 *
 * **That breakpoint is 1180px, not 1024, and the number came from the products table.** A rail is
 * 15.5rem the content column does not get, and at 1024 that left the table 693px — where "נוסף
 * בתאריך" and the product name itself no longer fit their own headings (measured on a build,
 * 2026-08-22). A tablet gets the drawer instead, which is what every dashboard of this shape does
 * and which hands those 248px straight back to the table.
 */
---

<div class="dash-nav-overlay" id="dash-nav-overlay" aria-hidden="true"></div>

<script is:inline>
  (function () {
    var card = document.getElementById('dash-main-card');
    var trigger = document.getElementById('dash-nav-trigger');
    var scrim = document.getElementById('dash-nav-overlay');
    var dot = document.getElementById('dash-nav-trigger-dot');
    if (!card || !trigger || !scrim) return;

    /**
     * **The tab list is looked up on demand, not once at the top, and that is not a style choice.**
     * This component is rendered where its scrim belongs, which is inside the card and therefore
     * before `.dash-tabs` has finished parsing. An inline script runs the instant the parser
     * reaches it, so a `getElementById('dash-nav-list')` here returns null, and the guard above
     * then returned before binding anything: the drawer opened for nobody, with no error (owner,
     * 2026-08-22: *"במובייל התפריט צד לא נפתח שום דבר לא קורה"*).
     * Resolving it per call costs nothing a person can measure and cannot go stale — which also
     * covers the panel loader, since a swapped panel never replaces the strip but a future one
     * might. The TRIGGER needs none of this: it is in the site header, which the parser has been
     * past for a while by the time this runs.
     */
    function navList() { return document.getElementById('dash-nav-list'); }
    /** The element that IS the drawer — the rail, which holds the store block and the list. */
    function rail() { return document.querySelector('.dash-rail'); }

    function isDrawer() {
      // The drawer exists only below the rail's breakpoint. Asked of the DOM rather than of a
      // remembered number, so one media query in dashboard.css stays the single definition.
      var el = rail();
      return !!el && getComputedStyle(el).position === 'fixed';
    }

    function setOpen(open) {
      card.toggleAttribute('data-nav-open', open);
      scrim.classList.toggle('is-open', open);
      trigger.setAttribute('aria-expanded', open ? 'true' : 'false');
      var list = navList();
      if (open) {
        var active = list && (list.querySelector('.dash-tab--active') || list.querySelector('[role="tab"]'));
        if (active) active.focus();
      } else {
        // **Back to the trigger, always — not to whatever had focus when the drawer opened.**
        // That was the first shape, and it only worked by accident: it read `document.activeElement`
        // on open and relied on the browser having focused the button as part of the click. Chrome
        // does; **Safari does not focus a `<button>` on click**, and jsdom does not either, which
        // is how the test found it. There the remembered element was `<body>`, `body.focus()` is a
        // no-op, and focus stayed on a tab inside a drawer that is now `visibility:hidden` — a
        // keyboard or screen-reader user left on an element that is not on the screen.
        // The trigger is the honest answer in every case anyway: it is the only way this opens.
        // Unless it is not on screen: crossing the breakpoint closes the drawer too, and at that
        // width the trigger is `display:none`, where a focus() call would silently drop focus to
        // the document instead of leaving it where the seller put it.
        // `display`, not `offsetParent`: the latter is the usual idiom and it is always null
        // without a layout engine, so it would have quietly skipped this in every test too.
        if (getComputedStyle(trigger).display !== 'none') trigger.focus();
      }
    }
    var closingTimer = null;
    /**
     * A close a PERSON asked for — the only kind that animates. `data-nav-closing` holds the panel
     * paintable for the length of the slide; the CSS has no delayed hide of its own, so nothing
     * else on the page can ever produce one (see dashboard.css).
     */
    function close() {
      if (!card.hasAttribute('data-nav-open')) return;
      card.setAttribute('data-nav-closing', '');
      setOpen(false);
      clearTimeout(closingTimer);
      closingTimer = setTimeout(function () { card.removeAttribute('data-nav-closing'); }, 300);
    }
    /** A close nothing asked for — crossing the breakpoint. Instant, and therefore invisible. */
    function closeInstantly() {
      clearTimeout(closingTimer);
      card.removeAttribute('data-nav-closing');
      if (card.hasAttribute('data-nav-open')) setOpen(false);
    }

    trigger.addEventListener('click', function () {
      if (card.hasAttribute('data-nav-open')) { close(); return; }
      clearTimeout(closingTimer);
      card.removeAttribute('data-nav-closing');
      setOpen(true);
    });
    scrim.addEventListener('click', close);

    document.addEventListener('keydown', function (e) {
      if (!card.hasAttribute('data-nav-open')) return;
      if (e.key === 'Escape') { e.preventDefault(); close(); return; }
      // Focus trap. The drawer covers the page, so Tab must not walk out of it into controls the
      // scrim is blocking — a keyboard user would be typing into a form they cannot see.
      if (e.key !== 'Tab') return;
      var list = navList();
      var items = list ? list.querySelectorAll('[role="tab"]') : [];
      if (!items.length) return;
      var first = items[0], last = items[items.length - 1];
      // The tabs use a roving tabindex (exactly one is tabbable), so Tab does not walk the list by
      // itself — the arrow keys do. Both ends therefore wrap to the trigger, which is the one
      // control that stays outside and must remain reachable.
      if (e.shiftKey && document.activeElement === first) { e.preventDefault(); trigger.focus(); }
      else if (!e.shiftKey && (document.activeElement === last || document.activeElement === trigger)) {
        e.preventDefault();
        first.focus();
      }
    });

    // A tab choice closes the drawer. Delegated on the document so it covers the synthetic clicks
    // too — the overview cards' `[data-goto-panel]` jumps go through `.click()`, and one of them
    // landing with the drawer still open would leave the seller looking at a panel through a scrim.
    document.addEventListener('click', function (e) {
      var target = e.target instanceof Element ? e.target : null;
      var tab = target && target.closest('[role="tab"][data-panel]');
      if (!tab) return;
      if (isDrawer()) close();
    });

    /** Carry the loudest marker still inside the drawer out onto the trigger. */
    function syncDot() {
      var list = navList();
      if (!dot || !list) return;
      var markers = list.querySelectorAll('[data-tab-alert]');
      var level = '';
      for (var i = 0; i < markers.length; i++) {
        // `hidden` is how a count of zero is expressed on these markers, and an empty badge must
        // not raise a dot.
        if (markers[i].hidden || markers[i].closest('[hidden]')) continue;
        if (markers[i].getAttribute('data-tab-alert') === 'danger') { level = 'danger'; break; }
        level = level || 'warning';
      }
      dot.hidden = !level;
      dot.setAttribute('data-level', level || 'danger');
    }
    // Deferred to DOM-ready for the same reason `navList()` exists: the list this reads is not
    // parsed yet. Everything above binds a LISTENER, which can be attached before its target
    // exists; this one has to read the DOM now, so it is the one thing that has to wait.
    function startDotSync() {
      syncDot();
      // The counts move while the page is open (an order arrives, stock is edited), and every one
      // of those writes lands inside this list.
      var list = navList();
      if (list && typeof MutationObserver !== 'undefined') {
        new MutationObserver(syncDot).observe(list, { subtree: true, childList: true, attributes: true });
      }
    }
    if (document.readyState === 'loading') document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', startDotSync);
    else startDotSync();


    // Crossing the breakpoint with the drawer open would leave the rail pinned open and the scrim
    // over the page, with no visible control to dismiss it.
    if (window.matchMedia) {
      var mq = window.matchMedia('(min-width: 1180px)');
      // Crossing the breakpoint is not a gesture: nothing may animate, and an open drawer must
      // not be left pinned open with the scrim over a page that now has no control to dismiss it.
      mq.addEventListener('change', closeInstantly);
    }
  })();
</script>
